; Willy's walking state transition table (16 bytes)
;
; Read by #R$8DD3 (at $8F65) after the keyboard/joystick has been scanned.
; Index = key bits + old state, where the old state is the byte #R$85D0
; (0-3) and the key bits are 0 (no direction pressed), 4 (a left key or
; joystick left pressed), 8 (a right key pressed) or 12 (both). The byte read
; becomes the new #R$85D0. Bits of the state:
; .
; bit 0: facing/direction, 1 = left, 0 = right (#R$9637 picks the sprite half
; from it and #R$8DD3 moves left when it is set).
; bit 1: Willy walks this frame (set at the start of a jump too).
; .
; Rows of four, by old state 0, 1, 2, 3:
; .
; +$00 no key: $00,$01,$00,$01 (keep the direction, stop walking)
; +$04 left:   $01,$03,$01,$03 (face left; walk only if already facing left,
; so a change of direction takes one frame without a step)
; +$08 right:  $02,$00,$02,$00 (face right; walk only if already facing right)
; +$0C both:   $00,$01,$02,$03 (state unchanged)
; .
; Observed in the simulator with tests/inputs/start.txt: pressing P in the
; Bathroom: state 0 -> 2; kept at 2 while P is held; released: 2 -> 0;
; pressing O: 0 -> 1 (turn) -> 3 (walk) -> 3.
@label=MoveStateTable
b$8421 DEFB $00,$01,$00,$01,$01,$03,$01,$03
 $8429 DEFB $02,$00,$02,$00,$00,$01,$02,$03
